Requirements: 9   with obligations: 9

[task-01] Build a set of labelled cases for the judgement "would this test fail if this
    defect were present?", where every label comes from actually running the test
    against the defect rather than from a model.
    -> labelled-cases-for-test-failure-judgement  [test_demand/explicit]
       Build a set of labelled cases for the judgement "would this test fail if this
       defect were present?"
    -> labels-from-running-tests  [functional/explicit]
       Every label comes from actually running the test against the defect rather than
       from a model.

[task-02] Each case holds the requirement text the test is meant to check, the test's
    source, the implementation hunk the defect was injected into, a text description of
    the defect, the exact edit applied, and whether the test failed with the edit in
    place. Cases come from two sources, and each case records which one, so the two can
    be scored separately:
    -> case-holds-requirement-text  [functional/explicit]
       Each case holds the requirement text the test is meant to check.
    -> test-source  [docs_config/explicit]
       The test's source is recorded.
    -> implementation-hunk-the-defect-was-injected-into  [human_review/explicit]
       Identify and use the implementation hunk that the defect was injected into.
    -> describe-defect  [docs_config/explicit]
       Provide a text description of the defect.
    -> exact-edit-applied  [docs_config/explicit]
       Report the exact edit applied.
    -> test-failure-with-edit-in-place  [functional/explicit]
       Whether the test failed with the edit in place is recorded.
    -> cases-come-from-two-sources  [functional/explicit]
       Cases come from two sources.
    -> case-records-source  [functional/explicit]
       Each case records which source it came from.
    -> separate-scoring-by-source  [functional/explicit]
       The case records which of the two sources it came from so the two can be scored
       separately.

[task-03] Tests run in the existing sandbox (`src/acceptance/execution/sandbox.py`). A
    test that already fails on the unmutated code is set aside rather than labelled, as
    `src/acceptance/mutation/baseline.py` does.
    -> sandbox-tests-run-in-existing-sandbox  [functional/explicit]
       Tests run in the existing sandbox (`src/acceptance/execution/sandbox.py`).
    -> baseline-failing-test-set-aside  [regression/explicit]
       A test that already fails on the unmutated code is set aside rather than
       labelled.

[task-04] Survivors include edits that change no behaviour. The build draws a random
    sample of survivors for a person to check, and its report gives the sample size and
    the share found to change no behaviour.
    -> survivors-include-no-behaviour-change-edits  [invariant/explicit]
       Survivors include edits that change no behaviour.
    -> random-sample-of-survivors  [functional/explicit]
       The build draws a random sample of survivors for a person to check.
    -> report-gives-sample-size  [functional/explicit]
       The report gives the sample size.
    -> share-found-to-change-no-behaviour  [functional/explicit]
       Report the share found to change no behaviour.

[task-05] The set holds at least 200 cases in all, and within each source at least 30%
    of cases are killed and at least 30% survived.
    -> at-least-200-cases  [invariant/explicit]
       The set holds at least 200 cases in all.
    -> killed-cases-minimum-per-source  [invariant/explicit]
       Within each source, at least 30% of cases are killed.
    -> at-least-30-percent-survived  [invariant/explicit]
       Within each source, at least 30% of cases survived.

[constraint-01] BugsInPy declares no license and this repository is public. Cases
    derived from BugsInPy are written only to a gitignored local directory and never
    committed; only aggregate counts and rates may be committed. The build reruns from a
    pinned list of BugsInPy bug ids, and rebuilding from the same pinned inputs produces
    the same file.
    -> bugs-in-py-license-and-local-derivation  [docs_config/explicit]
       Cases derived from BugsInPy are written only to a gitignored local directory and
       are never committed; only aggregate counts and rates may be committed.
    -> bugs-in-py-pinned-rebuild-determinism  [invariant/explicit]
       The build reruns from a pinned list of BugsInPy bug ids, and rebuilding from the
       same pinned inputs produces the same file.

[constraint-02] The cases load through a typed loader in `src/acceptance/benchmark/`,
